<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="possible-modifications" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Possible Modifications to the Six Dimensions</title>

  <p>
    We recognize that each after school program has a very different nature and different ways to
    achieve similar goals. Therefore, you may find that certain elements of the six dimensions
    don't apply 100% to your setting. However, we believe that most concepts are sufficiently
    general and can be applied to a broader range of programs with minimal modifications. It is
    also possible that you want one of the dimensions to have more weight than others, and make
    efforts to develop them more.
  </p>

  <p>
    The six dimensions were developed with a mathematics program in mind, and the context of a
    math program is prevalent in dimensions <em>3, Deep Critical and Logical Thinking</em>,
    <em>4, Relevance, Friendly Language and Representations,</em> and <em>6, Promoting social
    justice</em>. The first contains much of the goals of the program, thinking of math skills as
    a kind of critical thinking skill. This can be replaced by a dimension based on the goals of a
    specific program, with consideration for what makes that subject cognitively challenging for
    students and which core thinking skills need to be developed. Students' attitudes and
    perceptions of the subject can also be a part of this dimension.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dimension 3 covers the ways we show, communicate and discuss math with students. Relevance
    means tying the activity to something the students experience in their everyday life, and
    friendly language and representations means we should be accommodating with students using
    other languages, slang, or other informal language to answer questions and we provide multiple
    ways (modalities) to illustrate mathematical concepts. In other subjects or fields, this can
    be replaced with whatever makes communicating the subject concepts challenging, and using
    content pedagogical knowledge to specify which access points are useful to include all students
    into making sense of the learning situations. This may involve stories, specific manipulatives,
    simulations, the use of certain diagrams, and so on.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dimension 6 includes discussing how the mathematics learned can connect to social justice
    issues. This can be done in many specific ways such as:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><p>exploring fairness and equity using fractions and proportions (to make sense of unfairness in numerical ways),</p></li>
    <li><p>developing projects that involve numerical operations, geometry, graph theory, or other math to make social justice visible in concrete cases,</p></li>
    <li><p>modeling systems with mathematics that involve injustice depending on the parameters,</p></li>
    <li><p>playing and analyzing logical games that explore fairness and unfairness,</p></li>
    <li><p>using probability to explore social outcomes that are associated with privilege, and exploring data to identify lack of diversity and representation.</p></li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    In programs focused on a different subject (such as history, arts, biology, chemistry, etc.),
    much of the same ideas still apply, but, naturally, the particular ways in which the content
    subject connects and promotes social justice may change. We suggest the content experts find
    and keep track of those strong aspects that can naturally lead to topics related to social
    justice.
  </p>

</section>
